====Noun====
[[File:US $2 bill obverse series 2003 A.jpg|thumb|The [[obverse]] of the two-dollar bill ''(sense 2)'']]

{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|uncommon}} {{ellipsis of|en|Uncle Tom}}.
# {{lb|en|US|numismatic slang|rare}} A [[United States]] two-[[dollar]] [[bill]].
#: {{syn|en|Jefferson|Thomas Jefferson}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=12 June 2003|author=PossumTrot|title=Treasury to Print More $2 Bills:)|newsgroup=rec.collecting.paper-money|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250622143141/https://groups.google.com/g/rec.collecting.paper-money/c/jMjsQO_Tk1I/m/B6iGA5gFkHEJ|passage=The article on CNN.com says the Treasury has $96 million in $2s in their vaults. They are printing new ones just to assure a supply. The new ones will be 2003 series with the same design as now. Printing more will have no direct effect on whether they circulate more, but articles like the recent ones will make people aware that they are still available. I'm picking up another strap of '''Toms''' tomorrow, hopefully crisp new - at least that what I asked the vault teller to order.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2024|author=Megan Gorman|chapter=Introduction|title=All the Presidents’ Money: How the Men Who Governed America Governed Their Money|location=New York, N.Y.&#59; Nashville, Tenn.|publisher=Regalo Press|section=note 9|sectionurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=T5sUEQAAQBAJ&pg=PA129|isbn=979-8-88845-081-9|passage=For a fun history of the challenges of the $2 bill, check out St. Louis Bank Historian Mary Piles’ article at <u>https://www.cnbstl.com/about-us/news/the-history-of-the-two-dollar-bill</u>. Wouldn’t it have been fun if “'''Toms'''” had become more mainstream in their usage?}}

