===Etymology 2===
Variant of {{m|en|TOM|t=[[time of the month]]}} influenced by the name.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|euphemism|personification}} {{synonym of|en|menstruation}}.
#: {{ux|en|Sorry, maybe next week. Uncle '''Tom''' is visiting.}}
#: {{ux|en|Maybe later. '''Tom''' is in town.}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|curse of Tom}}

===Etymology 3===
Possibly [[onomatopoeia]], conflated with the given name, given the practice of giving objects such as [[w:Big Ben|Big Ben]] human names. Alternatively, it may derive from an inscription on the old bell used as metal to make the Great Tom of Oxford in 1680: ''In Thomæ laude resono bim bom sine fraude.''<ref>[http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=VBDnAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA405#v=onepage&q&f=true "Bells"], ''The Penny Magazine'', pp.404-406, 1834.</ref>

====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun}}

# A large, deep-toned bell, or a particularly notable example of one. {{defdate|since 17th century}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1857|author=William Chambers; Robert Chambers|title=Something about bells|journal=Chambers's Journal|volume=28|issue=207|pageurl=http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=1nhUAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA398#v=onepage&q&f=true|page=398
|passage=They had a thick rim, and when struck with pieces of wood, gave out a tone deeper than that of some of the Great '''Toms''' renowned in belldom.}}
#* {{RQ:Harper's|year=1857|url=http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=dIQCAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA172#v=onepage&q&f=true|title=An earthquake in Honduras
|passage=After these came innumerable little boys bearing little bells, which made little noises in comparison to the "Big '''Tom'''" that preceded them.}}
#* '''1825''', Moncrieff, [http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=xE5MAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA245#v=onepage&q&f=true "A Parish-Clerk was Johnny Bell"], ''The Universal Songster'' (in a song about a man who hangs himself in the bell tower):
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1848|titleurl=http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=3gUxAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA309#v=onepage&q&f=true|title=The book auction of New York|journal=The Literary World
|passage=The city [New York] does not know a better auctioneer; the celebrated '''Tom''' Bell not ringing clearer.}}

